Topological Sort
The code below determines if all courses can be finished given a list of prerequisites. It utilizes Kahn’s Algorithm for topological sorting. Here are some key points:

In-degree and Adjacency List Construction

indegree Array: This array tracks the number of prerequisites (in-degrees) for each course.
map (Adjacency List): This stores the list of courses dependent on each course. For each pair (a, b) in prerequisites, course a depends on course b. Thus:

indegree[a] is incremented by 1.
Course a is added to the list in map.get(b).


Queue Initialization

A queue que is initialized to store courses with no prerequisites (i.e., indegree[i] == 0), as these can be taken immediately.

Topological Sorting and Cycle Detection

Courses are processed from the queue, simulating the “removal” of these courses and their effect on other courses.
For each course processed, its dependent courses have their in-degrees decremented. If a course’s in-degree becomes 0, it is added to the queue.
A count variable is used to keep track of the number of courses processed.

Result Determination

If count equals numCourses, it indicates that all courses can be completed (i.e., the graph has no cycles and all courses can be ordered). Otherwise, it’s not possible to finish all courses due to a cycle in the prerequisites.


class Solution {
    public boolean canFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
        // keep track of the indegree and connected courses for each course
        int[] indegree = new int[numCourses];
        Map&lt;Integer, List&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; map = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; prerequisites.length; i++) {
            int a = prerequisites[i][0], b = prerequisites[i][1];
            indegree[a]++;
            if (map.containsKey(b)) {
                map.get(b).add(a);
            } else {
                List&lt;Integer&gt; lst = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
                lst.add(a);
                map.put(b, lst);
            }
        }

        // identify all the courses with no prerequisites
        Queue&lt;Integer&gt; que = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; numCourses; i++) {
            if (indegree[i] == 0) {
                que.offer(i);
            }
        }

        // keep remove courses with no prerequisites and their following courses
        int count = 0;
        while (!que.isEmpty()) {
            int num = que.poll();
            count++;
            List&lt;Integer&gt; connections = map.getOrDefault(num, Collections.emptyList());
            for (int next : connections) {
                indegree[next]--;
                if (indegree[next] == 0) {
                    que.offer(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return count == numCourses;
    }
}

Topological Sort
The provided code below solves the problem of determining the order in which courses should be taken given a list of prerequisites. The solution involves topological sorting using Kahn’s Algorithm. Below are the key points:

In-degree and Adjacency List Initialization

indegree Array: This array keeps track of the number of prerequisites (in-degrees) for each course.
map (Adjacency List): This stores the list of courses dependent on each course. Each course has a list of subsequent courses that depend on it.
For each pair (a, b) in prerequisites, course a depends on course b. Thus:

indegree[a] is incremented by 1.
Course a is added to the list in map.get(b).


Queue Initialization

A queue que is initialized to store courses with no prerequisites (indegree[i] == 0), indicating they can be taken first.

Topological Sorting and Order Determination

Processing the Queue: Courses are dequeued one by one, simulating the “removal” of these courses and their effect on other courses.
Result Array: The res array is used to store the order of courses as they are processed.
Updating In-degrees: For each course processed, its dependent courses have their in-degrees decremented. If a course’s in-degree becomes 0, it is added to the queue.

Checking for Completion

Completion Check: The count variable keeps track of the number of courses processed. If count equals numCourses, all courses can be taken, and the res array containing the course order is returned.
Cycle Detection: If count is not equal to numCourses, the graph contains a cycle, making it impossible to complete all courses. In this case, an empty array is returned.


class Solution {
    public int[] findOrder(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
        int[] indegree = new int[numCourses];
        Map&lt;Integer, List&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; map = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; numCourses; i++) {
            map.put(i, new ArrayList&lt;&gt;());
        }
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; prerequisites.length; i++) {
            int a = prerequisites[i][0], b = prerequisites[i][1];
            indegree[a]++;
            map.get(b).add(a);
        }

        Queue&lt;Integer&gt; que = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; numCourses; i++) {
            if (indegree[i] == 0) {
                que.offer(i);
            }
        }

        int count = 0;
        int[] res = new int[numCourses];
        while (!que.isEmpty()) {
            int curr = que.poll();
            res[count++] = curr;

            for (int next : map.get(curr)) {
                indegree[next]--;
                if (indegree[next] == 0) {
                    que.offer(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return count == numCourses ? res : new int[0];
    }
}

